summary:
  The cancel takes the run's token after the run counter has moved on, so
  the mark names a run that does not exist yet.

history:
  The counter is bumped in the cancel itself, so that the cancelled run's
  answer is recognised as stale when it finally comes. The token for the
  mark has to be taken before that. Taken after, the mark stops nothing
  that is running, and it sits there waiting for the next calibration:
  the one the listener starts right after cancelling carries exactly that
  number, reads the mark and quietly does nothing.

target:
  package/contents/ui/SyncEngine.qml

find:
          var gone = CalibLogic.token(_calibBornMs, _calibRunSeq);
          _calibRunSeq++;

replace:
          _calibRunSeq++;
          var gone = CalibLogic.token(_calibBornMs, _calibRunSeq);

expect:
  Killed by tst_syncengine::test_a_cancel_marks_the_run_that_was_in_flight.
  Line-neutral.
